WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.060 --> 00:00:07.800
- So my name is Rajit Manohar.

00:00:07.800 --> 00:00:10.920
I'm a faculty member
in engineering at Yale,

00:00:10.920 --> 00:00:13.830
and we design computer chips.

00:00:13.830 --> 00:00:16.320
This is quite a big departure from some

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:18.150
of the topics we've heard from before.

00:00:18.150 --> 00:00:20.490
And normally if I gave a talk

00:00:20.490 --> 00:00:21.990
that had influence in its title,

00:00:21.990 --> 00:00:25.380
people would be wondering what
AI hardware I was designing.

00:00:25.380 --> 00:00:28.140
But that's not what I'm
gonna be talking about today.

00:00:28.140 --> 00:00:31.830
View me as a consumer of inference,

00:00:31.830 --> 00:00:34.320
I have questions about whether

00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:36.360
something I'm doing is a good idea or not,

00:00:36.360 --> 00:00:37.830
and I want to see if that's true

00:00:37.830 --> 00:00:40.350
and how do we go about
evaluating these things

00:00:40.350 --> 00:00:41.700
in the design of chips.

00:00:41.700 --> 00:00:43.770
So, I'll give you a little
bit of background on the kind

00:00:43.770 --> 00:00:44.940
of things that we work on,

00:00:44.940 --> 00:00:46.740
just to give you some sense,

00:00:46.740 --> 00:00:48.240
because it's quite different.

00:00:48.240 --> 00:00:50.700
But it was interesting to
see the talks this morning

00:00:50.700 --> 00:00:53.250
because I see many commonalities

00:00:53.250 --> 00:00:55.080
and models that are being talked about

00:00:55.080 --> 00:00:56.730
in different disciplines.

00:00:56.730 --> 00:00:59.100
And you'll probably see
figures that look familiar

00:00:59.100 --> 00:01:02.010
to you from the talks we've heard earlier.

00:01:02.010 --> 00:01:04.080
So I'm a computer engineer.

00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:04.913
What does that mean?

00:01:04.913 --> 00:01:06.450
We design and build computers, okay?

00:01:06.450 --> 00:01:09.000
So, and this is actually,

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:11.790
most people think of
this as a very, you know,

00:01:11.790 --> 00:01:12.870
formulaic process.

00:01:12.870 --> 00:01:14.640
But I would argue this
is actually, you know,

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:16.240
I would argue it's more like art

00:01:17.130 --> 00:01:18.750
rather than science,

00:01:18.750 --> 00:01:20.070
because you are coming up,

00:01:20.070 --> 00:01:21.840
it's a creative process.

00:01:21.840 --> 00:01:24.000
You're trying to see if
something's a good idea or not.

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:26.490
And this design can happen
at many different levels

00:01:26.490 --> 00:01:27.630
of abstraction.

00:01:27.630 --> 00:01:29.940
And this is kind of,
you'll see this as a theme

00:01:29.940 --> 00:01:32.550
on how we try to understand
if some idea we have

00:01:32.550 --> 00:01:34.560
is a good idea or not, right?

00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:36.540
And you know, these
abstractions go all the way down

00:01:36.540 --> 00:01:38.400
from physics all the
way down to, you know,

00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:40.080
the software running on your computer.

00:01:40.080 --> 00:01:41.760
And at every level,

00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:43.653
there are many sort of
intermediate levels.

00:01:43.653 --> 00:01:45.330
This is all subdivided too,

00:01:45.330 --> 00:01:47.630
so that we can actually
manage the complexity.

00:01:48.720 --> 00:01:53.160
And throughout, even though
most people don't do this,

00:01:53.160 --> 00:01:54.690
I have a particular bias

00:01:54.690 --> 00:01:56.730
in the way we work on our systems,

00:01:56.730 --> 00:01:58.560
and we have mathematical models

00:01:58.560 --> 00:02:00.270
that we can use at many different levels

00:02:00.270 --> 00:02:01.623
of abstraction, okay?

00:02:02.460 --> 00:02:05.130
So, I mean, these are some
examples of things we do.

00:02:05.130 --> 00:02:08.107
At the end of the day, we design
something that looks like,

00:02:08.107 --> 00:02:10.770
you know, this picture over here,

00:02:10.770 --> 00:02:12.360
you can just think of it as geometry.

00:02:12.360 --> 00:02:14.700
It's lots of rectangles, different colors,

00:02:14.700 --> 00:02:17.220
different colors are different materials.

00:02:17.220 --> 00:02:20.070
You're really specifying
exactly what the manufacturer

00:02:20.070 --> 00:02:22.050
has to do to manufacture
the electronics, okay?

00:02:22.050 --> 00:02:25.140
So, that's the sort of things we do.

00:02:25.140 --> 00:02:27.030
And these are engineered systems, right?

00:02:27.030 --> 00:02:31.230
So, you think I have full
control at the nanometer scale

00:02:31.230 --> 00:02:33.780
of what every little piece is doing.

00:02:33.780 --> 00:02:35.250
So, this should be easy.

00:02:35.250 --> 00:02:36.570
It should be easy for us to figure out

00:02:36.570 --> 00:02:38.580
whether this is good or bad.

00:02:38.580 --> 00:02:40.620
It's all, you know, I have full control,

00:02:40.620 --> 00:02:43.170
I have software, I can
represent everything,

00:02:43.170 --> 00:02:44.340
no problem.

00:02:44.340 --> 00:02:45.840
But the problem arises

00:02:45.840 --> 00:02:50.013
simply because these systems
are extremely complicated.

00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:53.460
So, there are billions of
components interconnected.

00:02:53.460 --> 00:02:57.480
It's actually pretty astounding
what we can make today.

00:02:57.480 --> 00:02:59.610
Everybody takes this for granted.

00:02:59.610 --> 00:03:03.240
We can make systems which
have billions of components

00:03:03.240 --> 00:03:04.980
integrated at the nanometer scale,

00:03:04.980 --> 00:03:07.410
and they cost $100, right?

00:03:07.410 --> 00:03:10.080
Otherwise you wouldn't have a cell phone.

00:03:10.080 --> 00:03:11.760
So, that's the kind of thing we look at.

00:03:11.760 --> 00:03:15.060
And the question is, you know,

00:03:15.060 --> 00:03:16.260
this is the question I'm trying to answer.

00:03:16.260 --> 00:03:18.060
I have some idea,

00:03:18.060 --> 00:03:19.140
I think this is a good idea,

00:03:19.140 --> 00:03:20.970
but is it actually good?

00:03:20.970 --> 00:03:22.380
I'm trying to answer this question,

00:03:22.380 --> 00:03:23.760
and this idea could be anything.

00:03:23.760 --> 00:03:26.070
I have a new way to do, you know,

00:03:26.070 --> 00:03:27.810
my latest machine learning hardware.

00:03:27.810 --> 00:03:29.610
I have a new way to do my chip.

00:03:29.610 --> 00:03:31.980
I have a new way to design my phone.

00:03:31.980 --> 00:03:33.153
Is this good or not?

00:03:34.121 --> 00:03:36.900
And this is sort of the standard
paper is my card is like,

00:03:36.900 --> 00:03:39.150
almost every paper you
read looks like this, okay?

00:03:39.150 --> 00:03:42.063
It says we have this great idea,

00:03:42.930 --> 00:03:44.790
we implemented the original system

00:03:44.790 --> 00:03:46.860
without this idea and with this idea,

00:03:46.860 --> 00:03:48.900
and we got these two different numbers.

00:03:48.900 --> 00:03:49.950
One is better than the other.

00:03:49.950 --> 00:03:51.870
My idea is good, right?
(students laughing softly)

00:03:51.870 --> 00:03:53.700
That's all the papers.

00:03:53.700 --> 00:03:54.783
But you know,

00:03:56.880 --> 00:03:58.350
a lot is left unsaid.

00:03:58.350 --> 00:03:59.460
And this is the part where it

00:03:59.460 --> 00:04:02.042
starts getting interesting, okay?

00:04:02.042 --> 00:04:03.660
By the way, this happens
in many disciplines.

00:04:03.660 --> 00:04:04.680
I'm just picking on mine.

00:04:04.680 --> 00:04:05.513
So, you know,

00:04:06.730 --> 00:04:09.230
a lot of people don't talk
about what didn't work.

00:04:10.890 --> 00:04:12.870
And a lot more, as we all know,

00:04:12.870 --> 00:04:14.643
doesn't work then does work.

00:04:16.410 --> 00:04:19.500
A lot of people don't tell you what's bad

00:04:19.500 --> 00:04:22.080
about your great idea.

00:04:22.080 --> 00:04:23.239
In fact, if you do that,

00:04:23.239 --> 00:04:24.540
you have a very good chance your paper

00:04:24.540 --> 00:04:26.250
won't even get published,

00:04:26.250 --> 00:04:27.780
because you actually admitted

00:04:27.780 --> 00:04:30.210
that this isn't a perfect idea.

00:04:30.210 --> 00:04:32.100
And the other,

00:04:32.100 --> 00:04:34.770
the thing that is always hidden, I find,

00:04:34.770 --> 00:04:38.520
it's very, very rare
to find this is to say,

00:04:38.520 --> 00:04:41.940
you know, this idea is only good,

00:04:41.940 --> 00:04:43.590
because this critical parameter

00:04:43.590 --> 00:04:45.510
has to have this particular value.

00:04:45.510 --> 00:04:48.780
And any uncertainty there
is gonna blow it all up.

00:04:48.780 --> 00:04:50.250
You know, I had this fun experiment

00:04:50.250 --> 00:04:51.660
when I was teaching a class one year

00:04:51.660 --> 00:04:53.370
where we were reading papers,

00:04:53.370 --> 00:04:55.950
and as homework I made,

00:04:55.950 --> 00:04:57.630
I picked a small piece of the paper

00:04:57.630 --> 00:05:01.590
and I asked a group of students
to replicate those results.

00:05:01.590 --> 00:05:02.730
That's it, nothing else,

00:05:02.730 --> 00:05:04.260
and then present the paper.

00:05:04.260 --> 00:05:06.270
So, they had to present it first,

00:05:06.270 --> 00:05:07.710
and then they had to replicate the results

00:05:07.710 --> 00:05:09.540
and then re-present the paper.

00:05:09.540 --> 00:05:11.670
And I can tell you that
not a single person

00:05:11.670 --> 00:05:13.500
reached the same
conclusions as the authors

00:05:13.500 --> 00:05:15.330
of any paper we had read, right?

00:05:15.330 --> 00:05:17.310
And these papers are,
you know, famous papers

00:05:17.310 --> 00:05:20.730
by famous authors from
famous universities, right?

00:05:20.730 --> 00:05:21.870
It was a very interesting,

00:05:21.870 --> 00:05:24.030
the students were just shocked, right?

00:05:24.030 --> 00:05:26.203
I mean, we all know this is, you know,

00:05:26.203 --> 00:05:28.050
I don't think anybody in
this room is surprised,

00:05:28.050 --> 00:05:30.000
but anyway,

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:31.410
but I think it was actually very helpful

00:05:31.410 --> 00:05:33.150
for the students to
understand the limitations

00:05:33.150 --> 00:05:34.170
of what's going on.

00:05:34.170 --> 00:05:38.310
So, what are the challenges in the sort

00:05:38.310 --> 00:05:41.310
of the world we work
on in computer design?

00:05:41.310 --> 00:05:43.617
We have a lot of tools
at our disposal, okay?

00:05:43.617 --> 00:05:45.240
So, I should admit that upfront, you know,

00:05:45.240 --> 00:05:47.670
this is an, as I said,
it's an engineered system.

00:05:47.670 --> 00:05:49.710
Usually we are not trying to do something

00:05:49.710 --> 00:05:52.410
that might like, you know,
might create controversy

00:05:52.410 --> 00:05:54.660
in sort of the physical
laws or anything like that.

00:05:54.660 --> 00:05:56.820
We're very far away from that.

00:05:56.820 --> 00:05:58.680
We're typically looking
at classical systems.

00:05:58.680 --> 00:06:01.080
Even the ones which have quantum effects.

00:06:01.080 --> 00:06:03.363
They have understood models.

00:06:04.680 --> 00:06:07.380
We can run simulations at
varying levels of detail, okay?

00:06:07.380 --> 00:06:09.600
But I can tell you
that, you know, there's,

00:06:09.600 --> 00:06:12.210
depending on what level
of detail you want,

00:06:12.210 --> 00:06:16.890
the execution runtime is 8, 9, 10 orders

00:06:16.890 --> 00:06:18.180
of magnitude slower, okay?

00:06:18.180 --> 00:06:20.250
So, there's just no way you can

00:06:20.250 --> 00:06:24.030
do a first principles calculation

00:06:24.030 --> 00:06:25.683
to compute what you need to know, okay?

00:06:25.683 --> 00:06:27.723
It's just not even possible.

00:06:29.760 --> 00:06:31.890
By the way, that eight
orders of magnitude includes,

00:06:31.890 --> 00:06:34.260
you know, running this on
the fastest supercomputers

00:06:34.260 --> 00:06:35.093
in the world, okay?

00:06:35.093 --> 00:06:35.970
So, you know, don't worry,

00:06:35.970 --> 00:06:37.320
the computers are faster,

00:06:37.320 --> 00:06:39.060
that's not a problem, right?

00:06:39.060 --> 00:06:41.700
I always used to joke that we
bootstrap the next generation

00:06:41.700 --> 00:06:43.710
of computers by designing faster computers

00:06:43.710 --> 00:06:46.050
that we can then use to
design the next computer.

00:06:46.050 --> 00:06:48.363
And so that's what keeps things tractable.

00:06:50.760 --> 00:06:51.720
Mathematical models,

00:06:51.720 --> 00:06:54.480
so there are lots and lots of
different mathematical models

00:06:54.480 --> 00:06:56.220
that are used to describe,

00:06:56.220 --> 00:06:57.840
and I'm gonna just pick one metric

00:06:57.840 --> 00:06:59.460
for this talk, which is performance.

00:06:59.460 --> 00:07:01.260
How fast does my computer?

00:07:01.260 --> 00:07:02.093
Just one metric.

00:07:02.093 --> 00:07:04.330
There are many others,
but let's just pick one.

00:07:06.030 --> 00:07:07.740
It's challenging to
capture all the details.

00:07:07.740 --> 00:07:09.390
So, the good thing about
a mathematical model

00:07:09.390 --> 00:07:11.490
is it allows you to try
to capture the essence

00:07:11.490 --> 00:07:14.880
of what you're trying to
model and ignore details.

00:07:14.880 --> 00:07:16.980
But the details sometimes do matter.

00:07:16.980 --> 00:07:18.540
And then you have to sort of figure out

00:07:18.540 --> 00:07:19.530
what's good or what's bad, okay?

00:07:19.530 --> 00:07:22.110
So, and I hate to say this,

00:07:22.110 --> 00:07:23.310
but this is definitely true.

00:07:23.310 --> 00:07:25.290
There are actually
like, you know, sections

00:07:25.290 --> 00:07:27.930
of conferences devoted to models

00:07:27.930 --> 00:07:29.430
where there's a large community that

00:07:29.430 --> 00:07:30.477
writes hundreds of papers a year

00:07:30.477 --> 00:07:32.730
and the models are completely wrong.

00:07:32.730 --> 00:07:33.600
Well, they're not wrong,

00:07:33.600 --> 00:07:35.040
they just don't correspond to reality.

00:07:35.040 --> 00:07:38.370
So, and they actually never did, okay?

00:07:38.370 --> 00:07:39.780
So, it's very interesting.

00:07:39.780 --> 00:07:41.630
But you can prove very nice theorems,

00:07:43.200 --> 00:07:44.100
which is a very, you know,

00:07:44.100 --> 00:07:46.950
that's why the academic
community sort of works it.

00:07:46.950 --> 00:07:48.750
And then the last thing
you can do is you can

00:07:48.750 --> 00:07:52.110
actually build the system
and see is this even good?

00:07:52.110 --> 00:07:55.680
The problem is, this is
a huge undertaking, okay?

00:07:55.680 --> 00:07:57.780
Just to give you some
calibration, you know,

00:07:57.780 --> 00:08:01.470
if you can know these numbers
when companies fail, okay?

00:08:01.470 --> 00:08:04.830
So, companies like Intel
have announced large projects

00:08:04.830 --> 00:08:07.050
that they canceled and, you know,

00:08:07.050 --> 00:08:09.420
take a $5 billion hit
on their balance sheet

00:08:09.420 --> 00:08:11.310
for one chip, right?

00:08:11.310 --> 00:08:13.650
So, these are extremely expensive prop,

00:08:13.650 --> 00:08:15.240
it's an extremely expensive proposition.

00:08:15.240 --> 00:08:18.450
And the problem is systems are complex.

00:08:18.450 --> 00:08:22.950
No one has enough space to
describe all the details

00:08:22.950 --> 00:08:25.800
to even do a reproducible
experiment, right?

00:08:25.800 --> 00:08:27.090
Even though it's an engineered system,

00:08:27.090 --> 00:08:28.440
somebody has a model somewhere,

00:08:28.440 --> 00:08:30.510
they don't want to tell you what it is.

00:08:30.510 --> 00:08:32.970
So, the other part of this
problem in this particular domain

00:08:32.970 --> 00:08:35.820
is companies are making a lot of money,

00:08:35.820 --> 00:08:39.060
so they don't actually want
to tell you what they did.

00:08:39.060 --> 00:08:39.893
So that's a problem.

00:08:39.893 --> 00:08:41.810
So, there's a lot of hidden information,

00:08:43.680 --> 00:08:44.940
but the good news is

00:08:44.940 --> 00:08:46.860
because there's a lot of money at stake,

00:08:46.860 --> 00:08:49.530
when people build these
models and they write papers,

00:08:49.530 --> 00:08:51.360
they actually have a very good incentive

00:08:51.360 --> 00:08:53.190
to get them right, okay?

00:08:53.190 --> 00:08:54.360
Because if they're wrong,

00:08:54.360 --> 00:08:55.530
I mean, this has actually happened.

00:08:55.530 --> 00:08:57.720
There are major companies
that went out of business,

00:08:57.720 --> 00:09:00.300
because the models they
were using were wrong, okay?

00:09:00.300 --> 00:09:05.038
It happened, well, 10
years ago, for example.

00:09:05.038 --> 00:09:07.440
So, there's good and bad, but I mean,

00:09:07.440 --> 00:09:08.790
but if you look at the complexity

00:09:08.790 --> 00:09:10.690
of the systems we are trying to model,

00:09:12.885 --> 00:09:14.970
I'm now simplifying things a bit.

00:09:14.970 --> 00:09:19.080
We've got about 10 billion components at,

00:09:19.080 --> 00:09:22.500
you know, individual device level.

00:09:22.500 --> 00:09:24.300
By the way, you could go,

00:09:24.300 --> 00:09:26.370
you could go to the
materials and the physics,

00:09:26.370 --> 00:09:28.110
I'm not even going there, okay?

00:09:28.110 --> 00:09:29.973
That's even much more complicated.

00:09:31.410 --> 00:09:32.880
And even if you get to like, the scale

00:09:32.880 --> 00:09:35.580
of a single chip in the
most simplified model,

00:09:35.580 --> 00:09:38.640
you'd have about 1,000
to 10,000 components

00:09:38.640 --> 00:09:39.780
all operating concurrently,

00:09:39.780 --> 00:09:41.190
all with some complexity in terms

00:09:41.190 --> 00:09:43.320
of the internal state, transitions,
and so on and so forth.

00:09:43.320 --> 00:09:45.653
So, that's the kind of
systems we're talking about.

00:09:46.620 --> 00:09:48.570
So, let me give you a very simple example

00:09:48.570 --> 00:09:50.430
of what makes this hard

00:09:50.430 --> 00:09:52.530
and show you sort of
an example of what we,

00:09:52.530 --> 00:09:56.220
the kind of things we do to
try to model these systems.

00:09:56.220 --> 00:09:58.050
So, here is a simple sort of,

00:09:58.050 --> 00:09:59.820
there's a standard compute pipeline, okay?

00:09:59.820 --> 00:10:01.800
So, what is this picture trying to show?

00:10:01.800 --> 00:10:03.030
I have inputs coming in,

00:10:03.030 --> 00:10:05.550
and then there are these different steps

00:10:05.550 --> 00:10:06.960
for computation.

00:10:06.960 --> 00:10:08.970
And the first steps
takes 20 units of time,

00:10:08.970 --> 00:10:09.810
and then you hand out,

00:10:09.810 --> 00:10:11.280
so it's like an assembly line, right?

00:10:11.280 --> 00:10:13.097
You hand over something
to the next step, right?

00:10:13.097 --> 00:10:14.610
It's a very simple pipeline,

00:10:14.610 --> 00:10:15.960
20 units of time for the first step.

00:10:15.960 --> 00:10:17.250
20 to the second,

00:10:17.250 --> 00:10:20.280
10 to the third, very straightforward.

00:10:20.280 --> 00:10:22.050
And I'm gonna take two
different variants, okay?

00:10:22.050 --> 00:10:25.020
One where is 20, 20, 10 and 10, 20, 10.

00:10:25.020 --> 00:10:29.493
And my idea is something that
will speed up step two, okay?

00:10:31.500 --> 00:10:33.180
I can look at the second system

00:10:33.180 --> 00:10:35.160
and say this is a great idea, right?

00:10:35.160 --> 00:10:38.640
Things got faster, but if I
apply it to the first one,

00:10:38.640 --> 00:10:40.140
nothing improved.

00:10:40.140 --> 00:10:42.390
The throughput of the
system has not changed,

00:10:42.390 --> 00:10:43.380
because you're still limited

00:10:43.380 --> 00:10:46.440
by this first step that
takes 20 time units.

00:10:46.440 --> 00:10:48.240
And so you think this is not a great idea.

00:10:48.240 --> 00:10:50.820
So, just depending on which
system you're evaluating,

00:10:50.820 --> 00:10:53.100
even though sort of locally you are

00:10:53.100 --> 00:10:56.790
improving some quantitative
metric, very clearly,

00:10:56.790 --> 00:11:00.150
the overall system didn't improve, right?

00:11:00.150 --> 00:11:01.680
And this can get,

00:11:01.680 --> 00:11:04.530
so this is the simplest
example I could think of

00:11:04.530 --> 00:11:06.930
because it's, you know,
easy to understand.

00:11:06.930 --> 00:11:10.440
But the networks of these
pipelines that are used

00:11:10.440 --> 00:11:13.560
to build these chips have,
as I said, tens of thousands,

00:11:13.560 --> 00:11:16.230
if not, you know, hundreds
of thousands of components.

00:11:16.230 --> 00:11:18.060
They're not this simple.

00:11:18.060 --> 00:11:20.250
Data routing depends on the value.

00:11:20.250 --> 00:11:23.430
So, for example, let's
take a very simple example.

00:11:23.430 --> 00:11:26.220
Maybe step one takes either
10 or 20 units of time,

00:11:26.220 --> 00:11:28.713
depending on what the input data are.

00:11:30.030 --> 00:11:31.080
Now, all of a sudden this

00:11:31.080 --> 00:11:32.880
becomes a very complicated question.

00:11:32.880 --> 00:11:33.990
Depends on the context,

00:11:33.990 --> 00:11:37.920
depends on what the actual
inputs might be in real life,

00:11:37.920 --> 00:11:40.260
whatever that means, right?

00:11:40.260 --> 00:11:41.790
If I run a program where it says,

00:11:41.790 --> 00:11:43.710
you running a program, you
might get different results.

00:11:43.710 --> 00:11:46.020
So, we actually can have a real difference

00:11:46.020 --> 00:11:48.990
in our evaluation of the exact same idea

00:11:48.990 --> 00:11:50.220
on the exact same system.

00:11:50.220 --> 00:11:52.050
I might think it's good and
you might think it's bad,

00:11:52.050 --> 00:11:53.940
and we are both right, okay?

00:11:53.940 --> 00:11:57.303
So, this is the kind of
challenge that we have, right?

00:11:59.520 --> 00:12:01.500
But you can say, well,
okay, I have this pipeline,

00:12:01.500 --> 00:12:02.760
why don't we try to analyze this?

00:12:02.760 --> 00:12:06.390
I have some, you know, logical description

00:12:06.390 --> 00:12:09.933
of the entire system in
some digital format, okay?

00:12:10.980 --> 00:12:12.600
At whatever level of detail, I have very,

00:12:12.600 --> 00:12:14.131
I actually have the level,

00:12:14.131 --> 00:12:14.964
I have the description

00:12:14.964 --> 00:12:16.830
at many, many, many, many
levels of detail, okay?

00:12:16.830 --> 00:12:18.090
That's kind of how we can sort of deal

00:12:18.090 --> 00:12:19.090
with the complexity.

00:12:20.130 --> 00:12:22.140
So, why don't we just analyze it?

00:12:22.140 --> 00:12:24.000
Well, it turns out that
if you assume something

00:12:24.000 --> 00:12:26.820
as simple as I can represent integers

00:12:26.820 --> 00:12:29.220
and simple things like addition,

00:12:29.220 --> 00:12:31.470
then everything is
immediately uncommputable.

00:12:31.470 --> 00:12:32.790
Like, you know, like because

00:12:32.790 --> 00:12:35.100
of Gödel's incompleteness
theorems, literally.

00:12:35.100 --> 00:12:36.650
So, you can't compute anything.

00:12:37.500 --> 00:12:38.730
So you can say, okay, well,

00:12:38.730 --> 00:12:41.190
that assumes infinite
precision arithmetic,

00:12:41.190 --> 00:12:42.030
all sorts of things.

00:12:42.030 --> 00:12:43.920
So, okay, things are finite state, okay,

00:12:43.920 --> 00:12:47.670
well, I only have 2 to the 10
to the 9 states to compute.

00:12:47.670 --> 00:12:50.220
So, it's just not tractable, right?

00:12:50.220 --> 00:12:52.260
So, these are the kind of
things we grapple with.

00:12:52.260 --> 00:12:56.550
And so sort of what we try to work on

00:12:56.550 --> 00:12:59.790
is how could we come up with models

00:12:59.790 --> 00:13:02.670
that have reasonable approximations

00:13:02.670 --> 00:13:04.830
where we can try to do some
of the things we need to do,

00:13:04.830 --> 00:13:06.540
try to answer some of
the questions in terms

00:13:06.540 --> 00:13:07.930
of evaluating ideas

00:13:09.300 --> 00:13:11.790
in a way that's, you
know, reasonable, timely,

00:13:11.790 --> 00:13:14.220
something that we can actually use, right?

00:13:14.220 --> 00:13:15.450
And I'm gonna show you example,

00:13:15.450 --> 00:13:17.730
an example of a very simplified model

00:13:17.730 --> 00:13:21.870
that's actually useful even
though it's wrong, okay?

00:13:21.870 --> 00:13:22.800
It's an approximation,

00:13:22.800 --> 00:13:25.650
but it's still, it's very helpful.

00:13:25.650 --> 00:13:27.750
And it was funny,

00:13:27.750 --> 00:13:29.100
there's a picture that looks very similar

00:13:29.100 --> 00:13:31.530
to this earlier today.

00:13:31.530 --> 00:13:32.490
These are models.

00:13:32.490 --> 00:13:35.220
The idea here is I've got,

00:13:35.220 --> 00:13:36.570
oh, I should just use this.

00:13:37.800 --> 00:13:39.030
I've got these little things here.

00:13:39.030 --> 00:13:41.253
This is B minus Y OPSI up,

00:13:42.090 --> 00:13:43.290
at the lowest level,

00:13:43.290 --> 00:13:44.130
well, not even the lowest,

00:13:44.130 --> 00:13:47.130
but at a reasonably low
level of abstraction,

00:13:47.130 --> 00:13:49.080
I have variables in my system

00:13:49.080 --> 00:13:52.380
that can take binary
values, zeros and ones.

00:13:52.380 --> 00:13:54.120
This says B went,

00:13:54.120 --> 00:13:56.460
B down, so B went from one to zero.

00:13:56.460 --> 00:13:57.780
It's with an event, right?

00:13:57.780 --> 00:13:59.070
That's very simple.

00:13:59.070 --> 00:14:01.470
And these arrows are trying to indicate,

00:14:01.470 --> 00:14:03.930
and you know, this is 'cause actually,

00:14:03.930 --> 00:14:05.100
we engineered the system.

00:14:05.100 --> 00:14:08.070
So, we know that we've
built some physical device

00:14:08.070 --> 00:14:11.100
that has B as an input and Y as an output,

00:14:11.100 --> 00:14:13.620
and B going down causes Y to go up.

00:14:13.620 --> 00:14:15.210
That's because there's
a device that literally

00:14:15.210 --> 00:14:17.580
implements this particular thing,

00:14:17.580 --> 00:14:18.413
function, feature.

00:14:18.413 --> 00:14:19.560
So, you can draw these arrows,

00:14:19.560 --> 00:14:22.410
and now these two arrows here
are going to B + that says,

00:14:22.410 --> 00:14:24.690
I have to wait for both of these to happen

00:14:24.690 --> 00:14:26.310
before B goes up, right?

00:14:26.310 --> 00:14:28.560
So, that's the kind of system.

00:14:28.560 --> 00:14:30.330
And then this has a cycle.

00:14:30.330 --> 00:14:33.750
But that's okay because you
see I've marked the cycle.

00:14:33.750 --> 00:14:36.210
The idea here is you unroll this into,

00:14:36.210 --> 00:14:37.950
you label all these events,

00:14:37.950 --> 00:14:41.100
this is the first appearance
of B down or B up.

00:14:41.100 --> 00:14:42.630
So, you tag everyone,

00:14:42.630 --> 00:14:43.770
every event with an integer.

00:14:43.770 --> 00:14:45.390
So, you can unroll this graph,

00:14:45.390 --> 00:14:47.640
but because, remember, I'm
building some physical system

00:14:47.640 --> 00:14:48.630
that I'm modeling,

00:14:48.630 --> 00:14:50.220
it turns out you can collapse this graph

00:14:50.220 --> 00:14:53.580
into a cyclic graph who's unfolding

00:14:53.580 --> 00:14:55.890
is actually the computation, right?

00:14:55.890 --> 00:14:56.723
That's the idea.

00:14:57.990 --> 00:15:01.800
And then, so it turns
out that these structures

00:15:01.800 --> 00:15:03.240
have been studied for forever,

00:15:03.240 --> 00:15:04.830
including in pure mathematics.

00:15:04.830 --> 00:15:06.150
If you're a mathematician,

00:15:06.150 --> 00:15:08.190
these are called max plus algebras.

00:15:08.190 --> 00:15:10.650
There are lots of theorems
in the field of dynamics

00:15:10.650 --> 00:15:13.170
and pure mathematics
that analyze these things

00:15:13.170 --> 00:15:16.200
to try to figure out when events occur,

00:15:16.200 --> 00:15:17.640
things of that nature.

00:15:17.640 --> 00:15:21.840
So, what we can do with
something like this

00:15:21.840 --> 00:15:24.690
is we can come up with
some theoretical results.

00:15:24.690 --> 00:15:27.690
Now that I have an abstract
model that says, for example,

00:15:27.690 --> 00:15:30.990
the model would say that the
time at which Y up occurs

00:15:30.990 --> 00:15:35.820
is the time at which B down
occurs plus some delay.

00:15:35.820 --> 00:15:37.200
Where did I get this delay from?

00:15:37.200 --> 00:15:38.760
That's an abstraction based

00:15:38.760 --> 00:15:41.400
on sort of the physics of the electronics.

00:15:41.400 --> 00:15:43.680
We know sort of the
time/temporal relationships

00:15:43.680 --> 00:15:45.690
between sort of those two events.

00:15:45.690 --> 00:15:47.520
And we abstract that as a number, okay?

00:15:47.520 --> 00:15:48.750
Again, this is wrong,

00:15:48.750 --> 00:15:50.253
but it's quite useful, right?

00:15:52.110 --> 00:15:53.850
So, that's kinda what we do.

00:15:53.850 --> 00:15:58.410
And then turns out that even though these,

00:15:58.410 --> 00:16:01.650
everything is running in
continuous time, right?

00:16:01.650 --> 00:16:03.450
You've got millions of these things,

00:16:03.450 --> 00:16:06.210
the graph for a normal chip would have,

00:16:06.210 --> 00:16:07.710
you know, millions of, you know,

00:16:07.710 --> 00:16:09.930
tens of millions of these nodes

00:16:09.930 --> 00:16:11.280
for at least to get to the scale

00:16:11.280 --> 00:16:13.740
where the analysis is useful.

00:16:13.740 --> 00:16:16.560
You'd have tens of millions
of these sort of events

00:16:16.560 --> 00:16:18.450
and they all have delay calculations.

00:16:18.450 --> 00:16:21.100
This is actually non-trivial
to do all of this stuff.

00:16:22.260 --> 00:16:25.173
But you can prove theoretical
results about the model,

00:16:26.124 --> 00:16:28.080
which then gives you a very good idea

00:16:28.080 --> 00:16:30.090
of how fast your ship is going to run.

00:16:30.090 --> 00:16:31.830
So one of the things
you can do, for example,

00:16:31.830 --> 00:16:36.000
is if you say, I have
the time for a transition

00:16:36.000 --> 00:16:38.640
or an event in this
system, i-th occurrence,

00:16:38.640 --> 00:16:40.890
if I denote that by time,

00:16:40.890 --> 00:16:42.030
so you can actually prove

00:16:42.030 --> 00:16:46.230
that the system eventually
becomes sort of periodic

00:16:46.230 --> 00:16:49.110
but not fully periodic,
periodic at an unfolding.

00:16:49.110 --> 00:16:51.870
So what this basically says is the time

00:16:51.870 --> 00:16:54.750
of the transition at some time

00:16:54.750 --> 00:16:57.060
at index iM + J

00:16:57.060 --> 00:16:58.577
is some constant

00:16:58.577 --> 00:17:02.310
+ M time another constant times i,

00:17:02.310 --> 00:17:05.190
so i and J are the two variables here.

00:17:05.190 --> 00:17:06.210
So you can see

00:17:06.210 --> 00:17:08.910
that all the transitions
eventually become periodic.

00:17:08.910 --> 00:17:10.470
So you can actually prove
this mathematically.

00:17:10.470 --> 00:17:12.030
It's not fun,

00:17:12.030 --> 00:17:12.870
but you can do it.

00:17:12.870 --> 00:17:14.223
It's a long proof.

00:17:15.180 --> 00:17:16.830
It turns out that there
are simplified versions

00:17:16.830 --> 00:17:17.663
of these graphs,

00:17:17.663 --> 00:17:19.080
like actually the one here where it's,

00:17:19.080 --> 00:17:21.900
I prove this in like
15 minutes in my class

00:17:21.900 --> 00:17:23.610
to undergraduates, so I can do that.

00:17:23.610 --> 00:17:26.430
But as soon as you start
removing the restrictions

00:17:26.430 --> 00:17:28.830
and getting these graphs
to correspond to circuits

00:17:28.830 --> 00:17:30.300
that you build,

00:17:30.300 --> 00:17:32.130
the proof is a lot more complicated.

00:17:32.130 --> 00:17:35.250
But we actually worked
this out not too long ago.

00:17:35.250 --> 00:17:37.080
So, that's the sort of thing we do.

00:17:37.080 --> 00:17:39.540
And then what we can then do is now

00:17:39.540 --> 00:17:41.970
that we have a mathematical formula

00:17:41.970 --> 00:17:44.070
for what we know the
system's going to look like

00:17:44.070 --> 00:17:45.390
in the steady state,

00:17:45.390 --> 00:17:46.950
I can figure out how
fast things are gonna run

00:17:46.950 --> 00:17:48.840
when every event happens.

00:17:48.840 --> 00:17:51.900
And I can now write software

00:17:51.900 --> 00:17:55.020
to take something that only
has a million in, you know,

00:17:55.020 --> 00:17:56.940
vertices and edges

00:17:56.940 --> 00:17:59.970
and tell me how fast my
chip is gonna run, okay?

00:17:59.970 --> 00:18:01.380
And this is actually pretty tractable.

00:18:01.380 --> 00:18:02.430
So, you can actually do this.

00:18:02.430 --> 00:18:04.197
We have software that does this,

00:18:04.197 --> 00:18:05.543
you know, as in the public domain.

00:18:06.420 --> 00:18:08.250
That's the kind of thing
we do for, you know,

00:18:08.250 --> 00:18:10.740
to try to figure out how
fast the system's gonna run.

00:18:10.740 --> 00:18:13.380
But again, you know, this
is an approximation, okay?

00:18:13.380 --> 00:18:16.350
A very simple change
you can do to this is,

00:18:16.350 --> 00:18:18.360
for example, B going down,

00:18:18.360 --> 00:18:19.920
the time at which this occurs is going

00:18:19.920 --> 00:18:24.360
to be the max of the two
possible predecessors, right?

00:18:24.360 --> 00:18:26.280
Because you have to wait for both of them.

00:18:26.280 --> 00:18:28.080
But that doesn't
correspond to most things.

00:18:28.080 --> 00:18:30.480
I could just, I could change
when one of my input changes,

00:18:30.480 --> 00:18:32.160
then I would get min.

00:18:32.160 --> 00:18:33.930
As soon as I add that complexity,

00:18:33.930 --> 00:18:36.540
there's an exponential
factor that gets added

00:18:36.540 --> 00:18:39.600
to the complexity of the
algorithm that you need, okay?

00:18:39.600 --> 00:18:41.580
So it's these,

00:18:41.580 --> 00:18:42.780
it's actually very interesting.

00:18:42.780 --> 00:18:45.480
So the reason we use this model
is not because it's right,

00:18:45.480 --> 00:18:48.570
is because it's actually tractable, right?

00:18:48.570 --> 00:18:50.220
So that's the sort of thing we do

00:18:51.300 --> 00:18:52.680
to design our systems.

00:18:52.680 --> 00:18:55.403
It turns out it's actually
quite useful in spite of that.

00:18:56.820 --> 00:19:00.840
There's another set of things
that we do to analyze systems.

00:19:00.840 --> 00:19:03.510
And it is funny that
there are the sessions

00:19:03.510 --> 00:19:04.860
called causality here.

00:19:04.860 --> 00:19:06.660
The technical term that's actually used

00:19:06.660 --> 00:19:07.830
in the literature,

00:19:07.830 --> 00:19:09.780
in our modeling literature

00:19:09.780 --> 00:19:12.870
is actually called
potential causality, okay?

00:19:12.870 --> 00:19:15.060
And the reason it's
called potential causality

00:19:15.060 --> 00:19:17.880
is because exact causality
can't be computed.

00:19:17.880 --> 00:19:19.053
It's too complicated,

00:19:19.950 --> 00:19:21.660
so you have an approximation of it

00:19:21.660 --> 00:19:23.850
that we call potential causality.

00:19:23.850 --> 00:19:26.310
And actually we have the
first results on this

00:19:26.310 --> 00:19:27.690
as it applies to circuits.

00:19:27.690 --> 00:19:30.480
This idea is actually
very old from the '70s,

00:19:30.480 --> 00:19:32.730
which applies to actually
distributed systems

00:19:32.730 --> 00:19:34.170
like computer networks.

00:19:34.170 --> 00:19:36.604
But we've actually got the equivalent idea

00:19:36.604 --> 00:19:37.440
that we use the same words,

00:19:37.440 --> 00:19:39.420
because it's basically the same idea

00:19:39.420 --> 00:19:41.370
when we are talking about
individual components

00:19:41.370 --> 00:19:44.280
of digital electronics
like signals and wires.

00:19:44.280 --> 00:19:46.260
And what you can do is very in,

00:19:46.260 --> 00:19:47.520
so the way you know if some,

00:19:47.520 --> 00:19:48.990
there's causality in our,

00:19:48.990 --> 00:19:51.750
in this kind of analysis
is sort of interesting.

00:19:51.750 --> 00:19:53.340
What we argue is that if,

00:19:53.340 --> 00:19:56.100
so this is a timeline,

00:19:56.100 --> 00:19:58.230
by the way, you can never
build this timeline.

00:19:58.230 --> 00:19:59.130
It's too complicated.

00:19:59.130 --> 00:20:01.320
But you've got a timeline.

00:20:01.320 --> 00:20:03.330
I've got all the variables in my system,

00:20:03.330 --> 00:20:04.800
everything because I engineered it,

00:20:04.800 --> 00:20:06.150
I know every single variable,

00:20:06.150 --> 00:20:07.470
not just the inputs and outputs,

00:20:07.470 --> 00:20:11.070
but all the internal state possible.

00:20:11.070 --> 00:20:13.200
And then I'm gonna put a little dot here

00:20:13.200 --> 00:20:14.730
that says the variable changed.

00:20:14.730 --> 00:20:16.620
That's all, that's all that means.

00:20:16.620 --> 00:20:20.280
And so I can build a timeline,
a logical timeline, okay?

00:20:20.280 --> 00:20:22.180
We talk about what everything changed,

00:20:23.040 --> 00:20:26.100
and then I can pick a particular variable

00:20:26.100 --> 00:20:28.320
at a particular point in time,

00:20:28.320 --> 00:20:30.900
and say what caused this variable

00:20:30.900 --> 00:20:33.240
to have this value at this time?

00:20:33.240 --> 00:20:36.270
And because I have the full topology

00:20:36.270 --> 00:20:38.160
of my engineered system,

00:20:38.160 --> 00:20:39.750
I can actually say, okay, well,

00:20:39.750 --> 00:20:41.970
the first thing I can tell you is the fact

00:20:41.970 --> 00:20:44.370
that this variable was
at a particular value

00:20:44.370 --> 00:20:47.490
at this time depends on
its own history, right?

00:20:47.490 --> 00:20:51.060
So that's what this
horizontal gray bar is,

00:20:51.060 --> 00:20:53.940
sorry, the one, this
one right here, right?

00:20:53.940 --> 00:20:55.110
The one with X.

00:20:55.110 --> 00:20:57.510
And then at this point it changed

00:20:57.510 --> 00:20:59.760
and this changed from that
sort of that previous graph

00:20:59.760 --> 00:21:02.610
came from these two other
variables having certain values.

00:21:02.610 --> 00:21:06.000
So I depend on my neighbors at
that previous point in time.

00:21:06.000 --> 00:21:08.220
They depend on their own
history and so on and so forth.

00:21:08.220 --> 00:21:11.880
So I can actually build a
cone of dependencies, okay?

00:21:11.880 --> 00:21:13.530
That's kind of how we say what what does,

00:21:13.530 --> 00:21:15.870
and this is potential
causality because as I

00:21:15.870 --> 00:21:18.840
said it's an approximate
approximation, right?

00:21:18.840 --> 00:21:21.390
And then the reason we can,

00:21:21.390 --> 00:21:23.190
we say that this is potential causality

00:21:23.190 --> 00:21:26.730
is because you can now prove
mathematically in the model

00:21:26.730 --> 00:21:30.813
that everything outside that cone,

00:21:31.680 --> 00:21:36.680
you can reorder in time that
exists an alternate execution

00:21:37.230 --> 00:21:38.880
where this node would have this value

00:21:38.880 --> 00:21:40.680
at this particular point in time

00:21:40.680 --> 00:21:42.570
and everything else can be reordered.

00:21:42.570 --> 00:21:44.400
So that in fact it didn't change,

00:21:44.400 --> 00:21:46.170
even though it's actually changed.

00:21:46.170 --> 00:21:49.083
I could have had an execution,
but it didn't change,

00:21:49.950 --> 00:21:53.280
and therefore it's not
causally dependent, right?

00:21:53.280 --> 00:21:56.400
So we construct these alternate
executions in the proof

00:21:56.400 --> 00:21:58.710
to show that the reason the causality

00:21:58.710 --> 00:22:00.060
is limited to this cone

00:22:00.060 --> 00:22:02.880
is because everything
else could be postponed,

00:22:02.880 --> 00:22:05.100
didn't have to have happened.

00:22:05.100 --> 00:22:06.240
Does that make sense?

00:22:06.240 --> 00:22:08.100
So that's the kind of sort of theory

00:22:08.100 --> 00:22:11.640
that we use to argue
about what's dependent,

00:22:11.640 --> 00:22:13.470
and again, this is an engineered system,

00:22:13.470 --> 00:22:16.140
so I actually know all the edges, right?

00:22:16.140 --> 00:22:18.090
So people are talking about systems

00:22:18.090 --> 00:22:19.560
where we don't know
what the variables are.

00:22:19.560 --> 00:22:21.870
It is totally different world,

00:22:21.870 --> 00:22:23.870
but that's the sort of thing that we do.

00:22:25.446 --> 00:22:27.090
Okay, I have a couple of minutes left

00:22:27.090 --> 00:22:28.710
and so I thought I would just stop

00:22:28.710 --> 00:22:30.840
by just talking about sort of the approach

00:22:30.840 --> 00:22:34.740
that the field takes to try
to make sure that, you know,

00:22:34.740 --> 00:22:37.500
the results we get with
sort of more abstract models

00:22:37.500 --> 00:22:39.540
lines up with when you actually

00:22:39.540 --> 00:22:41.430
manufacture the device
and you know, get it.

00:22:41.430 --> 00:22:42.750
So by the way, just if,

00:22:42.750 --> 00:22:44.190
you may not know these costs,

00:22:44.190 --> 00:22:48.820
but to manufacture a chip
costs roughly $5 to 10 million

00:22:49.950 --> 00:22:51.360
to get the first part.

00:22:51.360 --> 00:22:53.800
And then every additional one costs $50.

00:22:53.800 --> 00:22:55.440
That's a very interesting economics, okay?

00:22:55.440 --> 00:22:57.720
So imagine if you're writing software,

00:22:57.720 --> 00:23:01.380
every time you hit compile you
have to pay $5 million, okay?

00:23:01.380 --> 00:23:03.480
So you don't do that too often.

00:23:03.480 --> 00:23:05.520
The funny thing is, in spite of that,

00:23:05.520 --> 00:23:08.310
sometimes it's cheaper for people

00:23:08.310 --> 00:23:10.380
to make the chip and test it

00:23:10.380 --> 00:23:14.430
than to run the simulations, okay?

00:23:14.430 --> 00:23:16.140
Believe it or not, okay?

00:23:16.140 --> 00:23:17.940
I used to always wonder
why companies wasted

00:23:17.940 --> 00:23:19.800
so much money fabricating these chips.

00:23:19.800 --> 00:23:21.420
And at some point I realized, oh yeah,

00:23:21.420 --> 00:23:23.850
it's much cheaper to do
that than to pay the people

00:23:23.850 --> 00:23:26.460
to wait for the simulation to be done.

00:23:26.460 --> 00:23:28.110
That's why, right?

00:23:28.110 --> 00:23:29.760
So it's a very interesting world.

00:23:31.560 --> 00:23:33.030
So at the materials level,

00:23:33.030 --> 00:23:34.830
I mean, you basically, by the way,

00:23:34.830 --> 00:23:37.050
all of these things are all underpinned

00:23:37.050 --> 00:23:38.220
by experimental data.

00:23:38.220 --> 00:23:41.040
This has been constantly gathered every,

00:23:41.040 --> 00:23:43.320
I would say every week, every month

00:23:43.320 --> 00:23:45.333
throughout the last 40 years.

00:23:46.350 --> 00:23:47.910
Companies are running examples,

00:23:47.910 --> 00:23:49.560
universities are doing research.

00:23:49.560 --> 00:23:51.150
I mean, there's a huge group

00:23:51.150 --> 00:23:52.290
of people working on this

00:23:52.290 --> 00:23:54.750
at every different level of abstraction.

00:23:54.750 --> 00:23:57.510
They collect experimental
data from materials,

00:23:57.510 --> 00:23:59.400
they validate that against
their physics models

00:23:59.400 --> 00:24:01.350
or vice rather the
other way around, right?

00:24:01.350 --> 00:24:03.870
They validate their models
with the experimental data,

00:24:03.870 --> 00:24:05.190
they run numerical simulations

00:24:05.190 --> 00:24:07.380
and make sure that they line up.

00:24:07.380 --> 00:24:09.000
Once they're happy with that,

00:24:09.000 --> 00:24:11.010
those models are then used

00:24:11.010 --> 00:24:13.710
to build the next level of abstraction.

00:24:13.710 --> 00:24:15.540
So you build models out
of the previous levels

00:24:15.540 --> 00:24:16.500
that have been validating

00:24:16.500 --> 00:24:19.560
against the lower level
physics of the materials.

00:24:19.560 --> 00:24:22.140
And then you build your
device, individual components,

00:24:22.140 --> 00:24:24.540
devices, again, these
are fabricated and tested

00:24:24.540 --> 00:24:26.523
and you know, all of this happens.

00:24:27.660 --> 00:24:28.980
Sometimes the next level,

00:24:28.980 --> 00:24:30.960
the whole discipline of
building these models

00:24:30.960 --> 00:24:32.460
is sometimes called compact models,

00:24:32.460 --> 00:24:34.980
because they're not first
principles physics calculations.

00:24:34.980 --> 00:24:36.150
They will be too slow,

00:24:36.150 --> 00:24:37.080
they're much more compact.

00:24:37.080 --> 00:24:39.000
They're a few parameters,

00:24:39.000 --> 00:24:40.863
well, few thousand parameters.

00:24:41.910 --> 00:24:43.800
Again, all of the numerical simulations

00:24:43.800 --> 00:24:45.360
underpin all of this

00:24:45.360 --> 00:24:47.760
at the lowest level of abstraction.

00:24:47.760 --> 00:24:52.020
At some point you go to, you
know, ones and zeros, okay?

00:24:52.020 --> 00:24:55.620
And at that point, you switch
from pure numerical simulation

00:24:55.620 --> 00:24:59.430
to simulations that have
delay values as real numbers.

00:24:59.430 --> 00:25:02.160
But then you do this as an
event based model, okay?

00:25:02.160 --> 00:25:05.430
So you record what are
the things, you know,

00:25:05.430 --> 00:25:07.290
input changes that causes
this output to change,

00:25:07.290 --> 00:25:09.783
so many picoseconds later
you record that, right?

00:25:12.387 --> 00:25:14.340
And then you scale up by saying, okay,

00:25:14.340 --> 00:25:17.250
I now have something
that adds two numbers,

00:25:17.250 --> 00:25:18.083
so now I'm gonna say,

00:25:18.083 --> 00:25:21.270
and block that adds inputs and outputs.

00:25:21.270 --> 00:25:23.790
Again, you are losing fidelity at all

00:25:23.790 --> 00:25:25.560
of these levels of abstraction,

00:25:25.560 --> 00:25:27.990
but that's essentially how we go about

00:25:27.990 --> 00:25:30.570
trying to figure out what's going on.

00:25:30.570 --> 00:25:33.810
So I hope I've given you
a flavor for the source

00:25:33.810 --> 00:25:35.260
of things we do and you know,

00:25:36.120 --> 00:25:38.880
how we try to make sure
that we are not wrong.

00:25:38.880 --> 00:25:40.770
And by the way, we are wrong all the time.

00:25:40.770 --> 00:25:42.570
I should just just be clear.
(Audience laughing softly)

00:25:42.570 --> 00:25:43.403
But,

00:25:44.526 --> 00:25:45.720
that's actually partially

00:25:45.720 --> 00:25:49.560
why there's very little big change.

00:25:49.560 --> 00:25:52.770
If you look at what's in your phone

00:25:52.770 --> 00:25:53.940
last year versus this year,

00:25:53.940 --> 00:25:56.550
the delta is extremely small,

00:25:56.550 --> 00:25:59.640
and the reason is if
you make a big change,

00:25:59.640 --> 00:26:01.440
it's like, well, I have no idea

00:26:01.440 --> 00:26:03.210
whether it's gonna work at all.

00:26:03.210 --> 00:26:04.650
And no one is willing to take the risk

00:26:04.650 --> 00:26:06.210
because, you know,

00:26:06.210 --> 00:26:08.400
many companies have gone out
of business by doing that.

00:26:08.400 --> 00:26:09.840
So it's a very,

00:26:09.840 --> 00:26:11.520
it's a sort of an interesting step,

00:26:11.520 --> 00:26:13.398
but with that, I'll stop.

00:26:13.398 --> 00:26:16.565
(Audience applauding)

00:26:18.326 --> 00:26:19.846
- [Priya] We have time for maybe one,

00:26:19.846 --> 00:26:21.404
maybe two questions.

00:26:21.404 --> 00:26:22.237
Yes?

00:26:24.930 --> 00:26:25.950
- [audience member] Yeah, it's a great talk.

00:26:25.950 --> 00:26:30.513
So when you prove something
about the higher level model,

00:26:31.920 --> 00:26:35.040
there's kind of an additional
proof which is required,

00:26:35.040 --> 00:26:36.720
which is about the relationship

00:26:36.720 --> 00:26:39.420
between the high level model
and the lower level model.

00:26:39.420 --> 00:26:40.253
- Correct.

00:26:40.253 --> 00:26:41.948
- [audience member] So it's one
thing to say that, you know,

00:26:41.948 --> 00:26:44.823
your high level model, you
know, is periodic or whatever,

00:26:45.900 --> 00:26:48.690
but the second connection
is often do you like,

00:26:48.690 --> 00:26:50.070
to what extent do you formalize that

00:26:50.070 --> 00:26:53.640
or it's just an intuition
as a system designer

00:26:53.640 --> 00:26:55.230
that this is more or less correct,

00:26:55.230 --> 00:26:58.230
to what extent can that
be made systematic,

00:26:58.230 --> 00:27:00.810
this relationship between
the different levels?

00:27:00.810 --> 00:27:03.003
- Right, so that's a great question.

00:27:04.590 --> 00:27:05.423
So

00:27:07.230 --> 00:27:09.042
when you switch from what,

00:27:09.042 --> 00:27:10.544
so there are two parts
of the question actually.

00:27:10.544 --> 00:27:11.610
I don't know if you intended it that way,

00:27:11.610 --> 00:27:13.080
but I think there are two
parts of the question.

00:27:13.080 --> 00:27:14.730
So I'll answer that in two parts.

00:27:14.730 --> 00:27:17.190
One of the levels is just
going from say materials

00:27:17.190 --> 00:27:20.280
to devices to bullion values.

00:27:20.280 --> 00:27:22.500
There's that part of the stack.

00:27:22.500 --> 00:27:23.460
And at that part,

00:27:23.460 --> 00:27:26.130
there's a pretty clear sort
of functional abstraction.

00:27:26.130 --> 00:27:29.520
There's basically two
injections essentially

00:27:29.520 --> 00:27:30.850
that I use to go from one

00:27:32.070 --> 00:27:34.350
information, representation to the other.

00:27:34.350 --> 00:27:36.800
And so those are being
crosschecked all the time.

00:27:36.800 --> 00:27:39.480
So at that point it's very consistent.

00:27:39.480 --> 00:27:42.600
Once you go from the low
level bullions all the way up

00:27:42.600 --> 00:27:45.123
to say, you know, a plus to mean addition,

00:27:46.710 --> 00:27:48.690
that's no longer the case.

00:27:48.690 --> 00:27:51.120
For that, we actually use,

00:27:51.120 --> 00:27:53.310
actually, we actually have
the first automated flow

00:27:53.310 --> 00:27:55.170
to do this, believe it or not.

00:27:55.170 --> 00:27:58.500
We'll see if the paper gets
accepted, most likely not,

00:27:58.500 --> 00:28:02.460
where we actually use a
combination of formal,

00:28:02.460 --> 00:28:04.380
like formal math, like theory proving,

00:28:04.380 --> 00:28:05.940
actually we use a combination
of theorem proving,

00:28:05.940 --> 00:28:08.760
model checking, and something
called translation validation.

00:28:08.760 --> 00:28:11.190
These are all three ways
to do formal verification.

00:28:11.190 --> 00:28:12.510
We need all three, it turns out,

00:28:12.510 --> 00:28:14.250
that all of them have
strengths and weaknesses

00:28:14.250 --> 00:28:17.280
and we kind of use them
appropriately where we can,

00:28:17.280 --> 00:28:20.490
we actually have an
end-to-end mathematical proof

00:28:20.490 --> 00:28:22.080
from the highest level of abstraction

00:28:22.080 --> 00:28:24.000
at which we simulate all the way down

00:28:24.000 --> 00:28:25.650
to the zeros and ones.

00:28:25.650 --> 00:28:27.600
But that's actually
like, I mean, as I said,

00:28:27.600 --> 00:28:29.130
the paper's submitted,

00:28:29.130 --> 00:28:31.200
but you know, it'll get
rejected a few times,

00:28:31.200 --> 00:28:32.970
I'm sure, but yeah.

00:28:32.970 --> 00:28:34.170
But yeah, that's a great question.

00:28:34.170 --> 00:28:35.870
It's a very hard problem actually,

00:28:36.780 --> 00:28:38.070
but we, yeah,

00:28:38.070 --> 00:28:41.520
because what we found
is the way we can do it

00:28:41.520 --> 00:28:43.770
is because the system is engineered,

00:28:43.770 --> 00:28:46.800
we track when we are designing the system,

00:28:46.800 --> 00:28:49.050
we keep track of side information.

00:28:49.050 --> 00:28:52.020
So all of these tools work by searching

00:28:52.020 --> 00:28:53.760
in some complex space.

00:28:53.760 --> 00:28:56.040
And this search is, you
know, exponentially hard.

00:28:56.040 --> 00:28:58.350
But because we know how we designed it,

00:28:58.350 --> 00:29:00.317
we can constrain it and
guide it in the right way.

00:29:00.317 --> 00:29:02.250
And so we keep the side information along

00:29:02.250 --> 00:29:05.160
and then you can do actually
generate a mathematical proof.

00:29:05.160 --> 00:29:06.690
Thank you.

00:29:06.690 --> 00:29:09.578
-  I suggest
we, thank you very much-

00:29:09.578 --> 00:29:12.745
(audience applauding)

